[issue_comment] kwikius | created=2016-12-19T10:49:39Z | updated=2016-12-19T11:03:16Z | https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/pull/5404#issuecomment-267935394
@khancyr 
You can use [std::common_type](http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/types/common_type)<T...>::type to find the converted type of the result
I added the cast in the body of the integral version to kill warnings about comparisons of signed v unsigned. Alternatively it could just not be allowed.
EDIT: I also changed to std:abs from fabsf for the floating point types. This allows use of double. I would suggest it would be a good idea to remove the restriction on only allowing float math

I would suggest adding some tests to just confirm this is working as intended, 
https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/tests
but I don't know where they should be added

```c++
template <class Arithmetic1, class Arithmetic2>
typename std::enable_if< 
    std::is_integral< 
        typename std::common_type<Arithmetic1,Arithmetic2>::type
    >::value
    ,bool
>::type 
is_equal(const Arithmetic1 v_1, const Arithmetic2 v_2)
{
    typedef typename std::common_type<Arithmetic1,Arithmetic2>::type common_type;
    return static_cast<common_type>(v_1) == static_cast<common_type>(v_2);
}

template <class Arithmetic1, class Arithmetic2>
typename std::enable_if< 
    std::is_floating_point<
        typename std::common_type<Arithmetic1,Arithmetic2>::type
    >::value
    , bool
>::type  
is_equal(const Arithmetic1 v_1, const Arithmetic2 v_2)
{
    return std::abs(v_1 - v_2) < std::numeric_limits<decltype(v_1 - v_2)>::epsilon();
}
```

